WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.880
Forests have always been a coveted resource&nbsp;
for humans. They also tend to be quite dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:07.880
and spooky. Maybe that’s why there’s a&nbsp;
long history of humans inventing monsters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:11.240
whose real purpose is protecting&nbsp;
the forests. In parts of Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:15.800
the shapeshifting Leshy guards the forest against&nbsp;
ill-intentioned hunters and harvesters. A Leshy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.880
helps you out of a tight spot—but only if&nbsp;
you’ve given an appropriate sacrifice and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:24.560
avoided disturbing the flora and fauna. And if you&nbsp;
don’t, they’ll trick you into harming yourself.&nbsp;

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:27.280
Then there’s the poisonous Umdhlebi&nbsp;
[oom-LEH-bee] Tree, or dead-man’s tree,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:32.080
a toxic plant in South Africa. According to the&nbsp;
Zulu people’s legend, the temperamental tree takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.280
nutrients from the poisoned corpses littering the&nbsp;
soil around it. The tree’s valuable fruit can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:42.000
collected only after it has already fallen to the&nbsp;
ground—a handy device for sustainable harvesting.&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.400
And let’s not forget the Mapinguari. A giant&nbsp;
Amazonian monster, this creature will literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.600
eat malicious trespassers—chowing down&nbsp;
on its victims slowly sometimes with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:54.800
the giant mouth in the center of its stomach.
The moral of all these tales? Don’t be greedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.920
with natural resources–and always keep your eyes&nbsp;
peeled in the forest or a monster will GET YOU.

